4.1 Procedure and Task 
 
After welcoming, participants were asked to fill out a demographic questionnaire. Thereafter, we conducted a calibration phase, in which participants entered text for 5 minutes for profiling reasons and in which retro-reflective markers where attached to the fingernails. 
 
The order of the conditions was balanced across participants. In either condition, participants were shown a series of stimulus sentences. For an individual stimulus sentence, participants were asked to type it as quickly and as accurately as possible. Participants typed stimulus sentences for 15 minutes in each condition. Participants were allowed to use the backspace key to correct errors. 
 
The conditions were separated by a 5-minute break, in which participants filled out a the SSQ simulator questionnaire [15] , the NASA TLX questionnaire [11] , the IPQ [21] spatial presence questionnaire and the Flow-Short-Scale [23] . After the final condition, participants were asked to fill out a self-assessment questionnaire, which was followed by a semi-structured interview. The experiment was carried out in a 90-minute session. 
 
Stimulus sentences were drawn from the mobile email phrase set [27] , which provides a set of text entry stimulus sentences that have been verified to be both externally valid and easy to memorize for participants. Participants were shown stimulus phrases randomly drawn from the set. 
 
An OptiTrack Prime 13 outside-in tracking system was used for spatial tracking of finger tips, the HMD, an external monitor and the keyboard. The monitor and keyboard were tracked for logging reasons. An Oculus Rift CV1 was used as HMD. The physical keyboard described in section 2.2 was used. The virtual environment was showing the virtual keyboard and a monitor resting on a desk. In line with previous work [7] , [8] , the phrases and the entered text were visualized both on the monitor and directly above the keyboard. 
