Statistical significance tests for entry rate and error rate were carried out using paired, two-tailed t-tests with a significance level of α = 0.05 . The analysis where checked for appropriateness against the dataset, log transformed data was used for error rate. Subjective feedback was analyzed with non-parametric Wilcoxon signed-rank tests. 
 
Entry rate was measured in words-per-minute (wpm), with a word defined as five consecutive characters, including spaces. The entry rate was 33.2 wpm ( sd = 20.3 ) for Fingertracking and 31.5 wpm ( sd = 17.0 ) for CapacitiveTouch. The difference in entry rate was not significant ( t ( 5 ) = 1.18 ,   p = 0.293 ) . 
 
Error rate was measured as character error rate (CER). CER is the minimum number of character-level insertion, deletion and substitution operations required to transform the response text into the stimulus text, divided by the number of characters in the stimulus text. The character error rate (CER) was 1.1% ( sd   = 0.7 ) for Fingertracking and 1.5% ( sd = 0.1 ) for Capacitivetouch. The difference in error rate was not significant ( t ( 5 ) = − 0.31 , p = 0.76 ) . No significant differences could be indicated for SSQ, NASA TLX, IPQ or the Flow-Short-Scale. 
SECTION 5 
Discussion and Conclusion 
 
Touch-sensitive physical keyboards allow for on-surface interaction, with sensing integrated into the keyboard itself. 
 
Our study indicates that writing on a touch-sensitive keyboard has the potential to be as efficient as typing using fingertip visualization, and, hence, external tracking or camera systems might not be needed to support text entry in VR, which could be beneficial for virtual office work on the go. 
 
However, the small number of participants do not allow to easily generalize those findings to the population. Also, it is unclear how typing proficiency could influence typing performance on a touch-sensitive keyboard. Please note, that our keyboard was not using the standard 104-key layout due to lack of availability of compatible PCBs. Typing on this smaller keyboard could result in absolute performance differences compared to a larger keyboard, but for both conditions. Hence, the relative performance differences (or the lack thereof) can be seen as transferable to other keyboards. 
 
Our work is a first step in making touch-sensitive keyboards available for VR text entry. If touch-sensitive keyboards will become commercially widely available they could become an efficient tool for VR text entry. In future work, we want to thoroughly investigate the performance envelope of touch-sensitive physical keyboards. 
 
